{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS introduces additional policy details to access denied error messages - AWS","context":"AWS has updated access denied error messages in scenarios where the resources are within the same account and organization. The new error messages include the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the specific IAM and AWS Organizations policy responsible for the denied access. This enhancement allows for faster identification and resolution of access denied issues, particularly in cases where multiple policies of the same type are involved. The error message now provides the ARN for various policy types, including Service Control Policies (SCP), Resource Control Policies (RCP), identity-based policies, session policies, and permission boundaries. This new feature will be rolled out across all AWS services and regions. For more information, refer to the IAM documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/additional-policy-details-access-denied-error/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"IAM Policy Simulator moves to the IAM console and adds additional capabilities - AWS","context":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) has updated IAM Policy Simulator, a tool used to test and validate IAM policies before deployment. The changes include moving the simulator to the IAM console, adding the ability to test service control policies (SCPs), and providing more flexibility for simulating scenarios. The simulator is now integrated into the IAM console, allowing users to test policies alongside identity and policy management. SCPs can now be included in simulations to test how they interact with identity and resource policies, and the API can be used to test how condition keys affect outcomes. New flexibility allows users to exclude specific policies for \"what if\" scenarios and receive per-policy decisions for identity and resource-based policies in cross-account simulations. These features help teams automate policy unit testing, detect over-permissive access, and validate guardrails with greater confidence. The updates are available in all regions where IAM Policy Simulator is offered. To learn more, consult the resources provided in the document","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/iam-policy-simulator-iam-console/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon S3 adds additional policy details to access denied error messages - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 has updated its access denied error messages to include the specific AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) and AWS Organizations policy Amazon Resource Name (ARN) for same-account and same-organization requests. This enhancement allows users to easily identify the exact policy responsible for a denied request and remediate the issue directly. Previously, S3 access denied error messages only included the policy type and reason for denial, making it necessary to manually inspect each policy to determine the root cause when multiple policies of the same type existed. The new capability applies to all AWS Regions, including AWS GovCloud (US) and AWS China Regions. Users can refer to the S3 User Guide and IAM troubleshooting documentation for more information on how to troubleshoot access denied errors in Amazon S3","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/s3-additional-policy-details-access-denied-error-messages/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-security","skill_description":"Covers AWS security services and workflows — Security Hub V2 (OCSF) findings, connectors, aggregators, automation rules, and security posture summaries; Security Hub CSPM (V1/ASFF) controls and compliance standards; GuardDuty threat findings; Inspector vulnerability findings; Macie sensitive data findings; Detective investigation; and Security Lake configuration and data aggregation. Applicable when questions involve security posture, Exposure findings, CSPM failed controls, threat findings, vulnerability findings, sensitive data findings, automation rules, or cross-service security configuration across AWS environments. Procedures use standard AWS CLI syntax and work with or without the AWS MCP server.\n\nServices: guardduty, inspector2, securityhub, macie2, detective, securitylake\nTasks: review, summarize, configure\nPersona: security-engineer, cloud-architect\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"aws-security"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on IAM policy evaluation order for access-denied errors."}

